# Supplementary Document A
## PCN Protocol and Network Terms 1.0

### 1. Protocol versus implementation

The protocol specification describes the packet format, addressing,
routing, messages, and the behavior of compatible implementations. It does
not transfer copyright in any particular implementation, nor does it grant
rights to the PCN Marks.

Rights to use the specification and to implement it should be described
separately. In particular, it should be determined whether an independent
implementation written from scratch, without copying protected code, may
be used commercially.

### 2. Private domains

Anyone may create private PCN domains to the extent permitted by law. They
may have their own local addressing, registries, routes, administrative
rules, and infrastructure.

A private domain does not automatically acquire the right to advertise
routes in PCN Global.

### 3. The official PCN Global network

PCN Global operators are subject to separate terms of authorization,
delegation of rights, routing, security, and abuse response.

The withdrawal of PCN Global rights should not in itself deprive an
operator of the ability to maintain an independent private domain.

### 4. No guarantee of operation

Protocol compliance does not guarantee the availability of routes, node
reachability, packet delivery, or the confidentiality of communications.
Operators are responsible for their own infrastructure to the extent
arising from the applicable agreements and provisions of law.

