# Supplementary Document B
## PCN Brand Guidelines 1.0

### 1. Ownership of the marks

The name PCN, PCN Core, the logo, and other designations may be legally
protected where rights to them belong to a designated entity. This
document alone does not constitute trademark registration.

### 2. Permitted designation of Forks

Independent projects may factually state that they use PCN Core code or
implement the PCN protocol. They should clearly distinguish themselves
from the official release.

### 3. Use of official designations

Without the appropriate consent, an independent Fork may not be presented
as an official PCN product, nor may it be suggested that it holds
official certification, support, or access to PCN Global.

### 4. Own identity

Authors of Forks may create their own names, logos, internet domains, and
visual identity, while respecting the rights of third parties.

### 5. Certification

If PCN introduces a compatibility certification program, the rules for
using certification marks will be published separately.
